\f2\b\fs40 \cf2 \strokec2 3. Measured limits of one CAS-versioned document\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf2 Physics first, as priors to verify: JSON parse/serialize runs ~100\'96200 MB/s, so a 1 MB document costs 5\'9615 ms per save; a heavy project (500 clips \'d7 ~1 KB + 2,000 registry entries \'d7 ~300 B) lands around 1\'962 MB. The real constraint isn't save latency \'97 it's\'a0
\f1 writer occupancy
\f0 : every completion-time registry merge rewrites the full document inside the single-writer critical section, so a burst of 50 completions means 50 full-document rewrites blocking every other mutation.\
Measure these, per percentile, on generated production-shaped projects:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Document bytes and parse/serialize time (p50/p95/p99) at 0.5 / 2 / 10 / 50 MB scales.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Merge latency inside the completion transaction (the number that gates throughput).\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Undo-history memory at snapshot-per-step vs. diff-based reconstruction.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 CAS conflict rate in dual-view (editor + shot mode) scripted sessions.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Version-history growth rate; compaction policy needed to bound it.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \cb3 Hypothesized action thresholds
\f0 \'a0(to be replaced by measurement): warn at 5 MB, act at 20 MB or when merge p95 exceeds ~50 ms.\
The evolution seam that preserves one-document semantics: split the\'a0
\f5\i physical
\f0\i0 \'a0layout into layered CAS objects (document core, asset registry, optional overlays) addressed from one root version. Semantically it stays one document \'97 one version counter, one save call, whole-document conflict semantics \'97 while a one-entry registry merge stops rewriting megabytes. Read-side compiled projections (for timeline rendering) are caches, not authorities, and don't violate the constitution. What must remain singular is the\'a0
\f1 versioned logical document and its CAS contract
\f0 , never the byte layout.\
